---
import PuzzleFrame from '../../components/PuzzleFrame.astro';

const steps = [
  {
    number: '01', title: 'Write a one-page brief',
    text: 'Name the audience, the story, intended age group, planned piece count, actual piece size, finished dimensions, material and target selling price. Add the table space, reference image and packaging you want. Choose one concept for the first proof.',
    deliverable: 'A brief with measurable choices and an unresolved-questions list.',
  },
  {
    number: '02', title: 'Design the solve, as well as the picture',
    text: 'Build recognizable landmarks, several distinct colour regions and changes of scale or texture. Decide where the satisfying early wins will be and where the deliberate difficulty belongs. Inspect a cut overlay at the intended size; do not leave every small piece with the same sky or pattern.',
    deliverable: 'Artwork plus a map of easy regions, repeated regions and fine-detail risks.',
  },
  {
    number: '03', title: 'Choose a material and a cut',
    text: 'Ask suppliers about board or wood thickness, surface finish, piece size, stock versus custom tooling and the available cut pattern. Compare a regular ribbon cut with a varied random cut. For shaped or wooden pieces, inspect narrow necks, fragile tips and edges on a real sample.',
    deliverable: 'A written product specification and the supplier’s exact template.',
  },
  {
    number: '04', title: 'Prepare the print files',
    text: 'Use the selected SKU’s finished size, bleed and safe area. Confirm accepted colour space, resolution, file format and how the supplier handles colour. Keep logos, labels and essential details away from trim uncertainty. Package art, reference poster and box panels are separate files with separate templates.',
    deliverable: 'Supplier-compliant files, an export record and a colour reference.',
  },
  {
    number: '05', title: 'Order a physical proof',
    text: 'A screen mockup cannot prove piece fit, print colour, glare, dust or box protection. Assemble the sample completely. Look for false fits, missing or duplicate pieces, lifting print layers, incomplete cuts, damaged corners and detail that vanished at final scale. Record the result before approving a batch.',
    deliverable: 'A photographed proof and a pass, revise or stop decision.',
  },
  {
    number: '06', title: 'Run a small, observable pilot',
    text: 'After rights and product-safety review, invite voluntary feedback on the image, grip, difficulty and reference sheet. Track damaged deliveries, missing pieces and returns as well as sales. Keep the batch identifiable and the support process simple. Change the product before committing to a larger run.',
    deliverable: 'A pilot record with costs, defects, buyer feedback and the next decision.',
  },
];

const supplierRows = [
  ['1 set', '$30.65'],
  ['30–49 sets', '$20.35'],
  ['50–99 sets', '$18.20'],
  ['100–249 sets', '$16.05'],
  ['500–999 sets', '$12.30'],
  ['1,000–1,999 sets', '$8.55'],
];
---

<PuzzleFrame title="Make a puzzle" description="A practical PointCast guide to puzzle artwork, print files, cuts, physical proofing, rights, safety, packaging and a small manufacturing pilot." active="make">
  <section class="p-section make-hero" aria-labelledby="make-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">PointCast Puzzles / The making guide</p>
      <h1 id="make-title">Make a puzzle<br />that wants to<br />be solved.</h1>
      <p class="p-lead">The artwork opens the box. The colour, cut and feel make someone stay at the table.</p>
      <p>Start with one original image and one physical proof. Give the picture, the object and the solving experience equal attention.</p>
      <a class="p-button" href="#workflow">Follow the six-step path <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a>
    </div>
    <aside class="make-hero-note" aria-label="The three design questions">
      <p class="eyebrow">Three things to design</p>
      <div><span aria-hidden="true">A</span><h2>The picture</h2><p>What story is worth exploring?</p></div>
      <div><span aria-hidden="true">B</span><h2>The object</h2><p>What feels good in the hand?</p></div>
      <div><span aria-hidden="true">C</span><h2>The experience</h2><p>Where does the next piece lead?</p></div>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section" id="workflow" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">From first idea to pilot</p>
    <h2 id="workflow-title">Six steps, with a tangible result at each one.</h2>
    <p class="make-context">This is a suggested development workflow. The supplier’s specification, a physical sample and the requirements for the intended market determine whether a product is ready.</p>
    <div class="workflow-grid">
      {steps.map((step) => (
        <article class="p-card workflow-step">
          <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">{step.number}</span>
          <h3>{step.title}</h3>
          <p>{step.text}</p>
          <p class="deliverable"><strong>Bring back:</strong> {step.deliverable}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section" aria-labelledby="physical-process-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Understand the manufacturing route</p>
    <h2 id="physical-process-title">Printing, cutting and packing depend on the product.</h2>
    <p class="make-context">Ask the manufacturer to map the actual sequence for your selected material and SKU. Specify how the image reaches the substrate, how pieces are cut, how the set is counted and how it is packed.</p>
    <div class="p-grid">
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Printed chipboard</h3>
        <p>Printify’s linked tin-puzzle example uses pre-die-cut chipboard and a glossy finish. A print-on-demand route may therefore begin with an already-cut substrate. Confirm the print method, coating and fit for the exact provider and product.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://printify.com/app/products/532/generic-brand/jigsaw-puzzle-30-110-252-5001000-piece" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Printify product specification</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Laser-cut wood</h3>
        <p>CreateJigsawPuzzles’ 503-piece wooden example lists laser cutting and two print routes: indirect printing laminated to plywood, or direct UV printing on plywood. Compare the sample’s edges, bonding, colour and surface feel before choosing the route.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.createjigsawpuzzles.com/design/irregular-cut-photo-wooden-puzzle.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CreateJigsawPuzzles wood specification</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Count, protect, repeat</h3>
        <p>Agree how the manufacturer checks that every set is complete and which package is included. Inspect both an assembled sample and a sample packed for delivery. Repeat the check when the material, cut, print process or packaging changes.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section" aria-labelledby="print-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">A print file is a specification</p>
    <h2 id="print-title">Get the final-size details in writing.</h2>
    <div class="p-grid print-grid">
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Resolution and colour</h3>
        <p>Use the chosen supplier’s requirement. CreateJigsawPuzzles’ linked 500-piece product lists a minimum of 150 DPI, RGB or CMYK, and a 3–6 mm bleed range. Printful recommends 300 DPI for its puzzle artwork. The exact product template takes priority; those figures are not interchangeable specifications.</p>
        <p class="source-links">Sources: <a href="https://www.createjigsawpuzzles.com/design/fi-8669.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CreateJigsawPuzzles SKU</a> · <a href="https://www.printful.com/custom-puzzles" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Printful artwork guidance</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Do the pixel arithmetic</h3>
        <p>If a chosen supplier calls for 300 DPI at a 19-inch square trim size, the image needs 5,700 × 5,700 pixels before adding that supplier’s bleed: 19 × 300. Increasing a file’s DPI metadata does not add image detail.</p>
        <p>Our concept gallery shows visual directions. It does not establish final-resolution print files, cut plans or approved proofs.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Test detail and glare</h3>
        <p>Print a full-size crop containing the smallest important detail, a dark region and a smooth gradient. View the proof under the kind of light where it will be solved. Compare matte and glossy samples if the supplier offers them, and verify colours on the chosen material.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Inspect the cut</h3>
        <p>Ask for a sample of the actual cut and material. Assemble adjoining pieces in repeated image regions to look for misleading fits. Check fragile shapes, incomplete cuts, edges and surface bonding. A decorative puzzle-line overlay on a screen is a design aid, not a manufacturing file.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section" id="rights" aria-labelledby="rights-safety-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Before a sellable edition</p>
    <h2 id="rights-safety-title">Resolve rights and safety early.</h2>
    <p class="make-context">Guidance sources checked <time datetime="2026-10-03">3 October 2026</time>.</p>
    <div class="p-grid responsibility-grid">
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Keep a rights record</h3>
        <p>Use original art, verified public-domain material, or a written licence that covers the physical puzzle, packaging, promotion, territory, quantity and duration. Copyright can protect photographs and illustrations; access to an image does not establish permission to reproduce it.</p>
        <p>Record the creator, source, permission, credit wording and files supplied. Review recognisable people, brands and third-party material separately. If commissioning art, agree who may manufacture it and who owns or licenses the result.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.copyright.gov/what-is-copyright/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Copyright Office: copyright basics</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Be candid about generated art</h3>
        <p>Record any generative tools and the human editing or authorship. The U.S. Copyright Office’s 2025 report says AI assistance does not itself prevent protection, while prompts alone do not establish sufficient human authorship. Do not promise exclusive copyright over an unreviewed generated image.</p>
        <p>Inspect the actual pixels for accidental marks, distorted objects and content that resembles someone else’s protected work before considering a commercial edition.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.copyright.gov/newsnet/2025/1060.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Copyright Office AI report release, 29 January 2025</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3 id="safety">Determine the intended age</h3>
        <p>CPSC identifies ASTM F963-23 as mandatory for U.S. children’s toys manufactured after 20 April 2024. The standard’s scope includes toys for children under 14; CPSC’s third-party testing and children’s-product certification requirements apply to products primarily intended for children 12 or younger. Applicable sections depend on the product.</p>
        <p>Do not use an “adult” label to sidestep a product’s actual design or marketing to children. A puzzle’s piece count alone does not settle its age determination.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/Business--Manufacturing/Business-Education/Toy-Safety" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CPSC toy-safety guidance</a></p>
      </article>
      <article class="p-card">
        <h3>Small pieces need an age plan</h3>
        <p>CPSC explains the under-three small-parts ban and warning labels for certain children’s products. It also lists exceptions, including articles made of paper. Review how the rules apply to the actual material, pieces and intended use with qualified product-safety support; a warning does not replace compliance.</p>
        <p>Request relevant test reports for the finished product and the selected inks, coatings and adhesives. Verify that the report scope matches the product and batch you plan to sell.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.cpsc.gov/Business--Manufacturing/Business-Education/Business-Guidance/Small-Parts-for-Toys-and-Childrens-Products" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CPSC small-parts guidance</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="make-context">The U.S. sources here are a starting point. A supplier’s assurance is not a complete market-specific compliance review; resolve requirements for each destination before a commercial launch.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section" aria-labelledby="packaging-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">The box is part of the product</p>
    <h2 id="packaging-title">Package for solving, storage and the journey.</h2>
    <div class="p-grid">
      <article class="p-card"><h3>Make the front informative</h3><p>Show the approved artwork, piece count, finished dimensions, intended age and material. Include the creator credit and relevant warnings. A clear reference image helps someone plan the solve; a larger insert can complement the box image.</p></article>
      <article class="p-card"><h3>Keep the set together</h3><p>Compare a resealable inner bag with the supplier’s available alternatives. Check the lid fit, box corners and whether pieces can escape during handling. Size the shipping carton and protective material using a real packed sample.</p></article>
      <article class="p-card"><h3>Make support traceable</h3><p>Keep a batch or lot identifier, production specification, count check and approved sample. Put a real support route on the box before selling. Decide how to handle a missing piece, a damaged box and an opened return, and budget replacements.</p></article>
      <article class="p-card"><h3>Describe materials precisely</h3><p>Record paper, board, bags, coatings, inks and transit packaging separately. Use documented recycled-content or certification claims only within their actual scope. Avoid broad “green” or “eco-friendly” claims without qualification.</p><p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/environmental-claims-summary-green-guides" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FTC environmental-claim guidance</a></p></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section" id="suppliers" aria-labelledby="suppliers-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Supplier research / 3 October 2026</p>
    <h2 id="suppliers-title">A prototype route and a dated price benchmark.</h2>
    <p class="make-context">These observations help frame a quote request. They are not quotes issued to PointCast, negotiated rates, endorsements or landed-cost estimates. No order or supplier outreach has been made.</p>
    <div class="supplier-layout">
      <article class="p-card supplier-benchmark">
        <h3>CreateJigsawPuzzles</h3>
        <p>Observed product: “Create Own 500 Piece Custom Puzzle Five Collage 19-inch Square Navy” (fi-8669). Minimum one set; 19 × 19 inches. The table uses the displayed $ amounts. USD is assumed for comparison; confirm the quote currency.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="CreateJigsawPuzzles observed per-set price tiers">
          <table class="p-table">
            <caption>Displayed $ prices, checked 3 October 2026</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Order tier</th><th scope="col">Displayed per set</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>{supplierRows.map(([quantity, price]) => <tr><th scope="row">{quantity}</th><td>{price}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p><strong>Scope:</strong> the default packaging is a poly bag. Board, finish, box, bag and insert choices can change the amount. Shipping, tax, duty, tooling, art and testing are not established by this table. Confirm a written specification and delivered quote before comparing a boxed retail edition.</p>
        <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.createjigsawpuzzles.com/design/fi-8669.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the exact product and pricing page <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
      </article>
      <div class="supplier-side">
        <article class="p-card">
          <h3>Printful custom puzzles</h3>
          <p>An on-demand route to investigate for an artwork proof. Its official page provides artwork guidance, including the 300 DPI recommendation. Compare the selected product’s material, dimensions, packaging and fulfilment destination before using its costs in a business plan.</p>
          <p>No fixed price or production lead time is claimed here.</p>
          <p class="source-links"><a href="https://www.printful.com/custom-puzzles" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Printful custom-puzzle page <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
        </article>
        <article class="p-card">
          <h3>What a useful quote must say</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Exact material, cut, piece size and finish.</li>
            <li>Quantity per design and allowed mixed designs.</li>
            <li>Box, inner bag, insert and packing scope.</li>
            <li>Proof, tooling and setup costs.</li>
            <li>Testing and documentation supplied.</li>
            <li>Delivered freight, duties, taxes and lead time.</li>
            <li>Defect allowance, remake and missing-piece terms.</li>
          </ul>
          <a href="/puzzles/business/">Put the quote into a business model <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section proof-check" aria-labelledby="proof-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">The quality gate</p>
    <h2 id="proof-title">Approve the physical object.</h2>
    <p class="p-lead">Keep a dated proof record. An attractive render is the beginning of the review.</p>
    <ul class="proof-list">
      <li><strong>Complete:</strong> Assemble the whole sample and reconcile the promised piece count.</li>
      <li><strong>Readable:</strong> Check small details, dark areas, gradients, colour and reference-image size.</li>
      <li><strong>Comfortable:</strong> Record grip, glare and handling feedback from the intended audience.</li>
      <li><strong>Clean:</strong> Look for dust, rough edges, peeling print, fragile tips and incomplete cuts.</li>
      <li><strong>Protected:</strong> Check the packed sample after representative shipping and handling.</li>
      <li><strong>Traceable:</strong> Save the specification, proof photos, batch identifier and defect decisions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Record material offcuts, rejected sheets, damaged proofs and packing waste as well as the finished units. A pilot should make those costs visible.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="p-section make-next" aria-labelledby="learning-title">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Design school × business concentration</p>
      <h2 id="learning-title">Use the puzzle as a small product studio.</h2>
      <p>Pick one of the ten PointCast concepts. Write its brief, audit the artwork, compare two manufacturing specifications and produce a proof-review sheet. Then use the business exercises to calculate a pilot budget, contribution margin and break-even sensitivity.</p>
      <p>This is a proposed learning path connected to PointCast’s University of El Segundo and IndustryNext context. It does not claim a partner agreement or enrolled students.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="make-actions">
      <a class="p-button" href="/puzzles/#concepts">Choose an original concept <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="p-button" href="/puzzles/studio/">Enter the design studio <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="p-button" href="/puzzles/business/">Study the economics <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      <a class="p-button" href="/ues/">University of El Segundo <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</PuzzleFrame>

<style>
  h1 { margin: .35rem 0 1.35rem; font-size: clamp(3rem, 6.3vw, 6.7rem); line-height: .98; letter-spacing: -.065em; font-weight: 500; }
  h2 { max-width: 24ch; }
  .make-hero { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.5fr 1fr; gap: 4rem; align-items: end; }
  .make-hero > div > p:not(.eyebrow) { max-width: 50ch; line-height: 1.65; }
  .make-hero .p-button { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .make-hero-note { padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.3rem); border: 1px solid #b4c6b9; background: #e9f4ed; }
  .make-hero-note > div { position: relative; padding: 1.5rem 0 1.5rem 3.5rem; border-top: 1px solid #b8cbc0; }
  .make-hero-note > div:first-of-type { margin-top: 1.7rem; }
  .make-hero-note > div:last-child { padding-bottom: 0; }
  .make-hero-note span { position: absolute; left: 0; top: 1.65rem; font: .95rem var(--pc-font-mono); color: #45624e; }
  .make-hero-note h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .make-hero-note div p { font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0; }
  .make-context { max-width: 75ch; line-height: 1.65; font-size: .96rem; margin: 1rem 0 2rem; }
  .workflow-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
  .workflow-step { position: relative; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .step-number { display: inline-block; color: #46634f; font: 1rem var(--pc-font-mono); margin-bottom: 1.7rem; }
  .workflow-step h3 { max-width: 22ch; font-size: 1.65rem; }
  .deliverable { border-top: 1px solid #d9e4dd; padding-top: 1rem; margin-top: auto; font-size: .9rem; }
  .print-grid, .responsibility-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .source-links { font-size: .83rem; line-height: 1.6; padding-top: .5rem; }
  .supplier-layout { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.2fr) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem; align-items: start; }
  .supplier-layout > * { min-width: 0; }
  .supplier-side { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
  .table-scroll { max-width: 100%; overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .table-scroll:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #185fa5; outline-offset: 4px; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  caption { text-align: left; font: .75rem/1.6 var(--pc-font-mono); margin-bottom: .75rem; }
  td, th { text-align: left; padding: .75rem .6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #d9e4dd; font-size: .9rem; font-weight: 400; }
  thead th { font-weight: 500; color: #fffdf6; }
  tbody td { font-family: var(--pc-font-mono); }
  .supplier-side ul { padding-left: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .proof-check { padding: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem); border: 1px solid #b4c6b9; background: #e9f4ed; }
  .proof-list { margin: 2rem 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem 2rem; }
  .proof-list li { border-top: 1px solid #b8cbc0; padding-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; font-size: .94rem; }
  .make-next { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr; gap: 3rem; }
  .make-actions { display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center; align-items: start; gap: .8rem; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .make-hero, .workflow-grid, .print-grid, .responsibility-grid, .proof-list, .make-next { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .supplier-layout { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } .make-hero, .make-next { gap: 2rem; } }
